# Hitchrail as a systemd USER unit. A template to copy and edit, not a file to
# install blindly: the paths below are almost certainly not yours.
#
#   mkdir -p ~/.config/systemd/user ~/.config/hitchrail
#   cp packaging/hitchrail.service ~/.config/systemd/user/
#   $EDITOR ~/.config/systemd/user/hitchrail.service     # fix ExecStart
#   printf 'HITCHRAIL_TOKEN=%s\n' "$(python3 -c 'import secrets;print(secrets.token_urlsafe(24))')" \
#       > ~/.config/hitchrail/env
#   chmod 600 ~/.config/hitchrail/env
#   systemctl --user daemon-reload
#   systemctl --user enable --now hitchrail
#   loginctl enable-linger "$USER"
#
# `loginctl enable-linger` is what makes this start at boot and survive logout.
# The unit does not do it, and cannot: it is a change to the machine's login
# behaviour and belongs to the operator, not to a file they copied.
#
# **A USER unit, not a system one.** Hitchrail spawns agents as you, reading
# your ~/.claude state and your projects. A system unit would want a `User=`
# and would invite running this as root, and Hitchrail is functionally a shell.
# A user unit inherits the right identity by construction rather than by a
# setting somebody can get wrong.
#
# Read docs/guides/phone-access.md before changing --host. The default is
# loopback and that is the safe answer; reaching this from a phone is a
# question about your network, not about this file.

[Unit]
Description=Hitchrail
Documentation=https://github.com/agigante80/hitchrail

# **There is deliberately no `After=network.target` here, and adding one does
# nothing.** This is a USER unit, and the user manager has no such unit:
#
#     $ systemctl --user show network.target -p LoadState
#     LoadState=not-found
#
# So the directive that stood here until 2026-09-06 was ordering against a unit
# that does not exist, and two rounds of reasoning in this file argued from what
# it "means". `server.py` records the rule this breaks: a guard that cannot
# execute is worse than none, because it reads as configured.
#
# The same is true of `network-online.target`, for the same reason. A user unit
# cannot order against a system target at all. If you need the network before
# this starts, the answer is #201 (socket activation), not an ordering directive.

# **The backstop for everything the exit code list below cannot name** (#170).
# `RestartPreventExitStatus=2` stops the deliberate refusals looping. It bounds
# ONE status, and an unhandled exception exits 1, which would loop by exactly
# the mechanism that ticket measured.
#
# These two are what make the default limit actually fire. systemd's default is
# five starts in ten seconds, and `RestartSec=5` spaces attempts far enough
# apart that the window never fills: 37 restarts in a row, none of them rate
# limited. Widening the window to a minute makes five attempts at five second
# intervals trip it, after which the unit enters `failed` and stays there, with
# its own explanation still the newest thing in the journal.
#
# **In `[Unit]`, not `[Service]`.** They lived in `[Service]` until systemd 230
# and are silently ignored there now, which is the worst possible failure for a
# limit: it reads as configured and does nothing.
#
# **#201 widened these from 5 in 60s, and the reason it is safe to widen is not
# obvious from here.** The budget does NOT keep a deliberate refusal stopped:
# a refusal exits 2 and `RestartPreventExitStatus=2` below means it is never
# restarted at all, so it never consumes an attempt. What this limit bounds is
# an unhandled exception, which exits 1, and a bind failure, which exits 3.
#
# **The widening was justified by a race that is real but was not the cause.
# Corrected 2026-09-06.** The claim here used to be that a unit binding a NAMED
# address races the interface coming up, and that this is what killed the
# service. The second half is false and the first half is thinner than it reads.
#
# The boot that was measured did not race anything. The adapter holding that
# address enumerated in under a second and then produced no carrier, no DHCP
# transaction and no lease for the remaining six minutes: it was plugged into
# the machine with nothing plugged into it. Two later boots had the adapter
# physically absent, and a third had it attached by hand 2m12s in. Every
# observed outage was missing hardware.
#
# **But one boot does show a real race.** Boot -3 of 2026-09-06 had the adapter
# connected: carrier at 35.172s, DHCP lease at 35.328s, and this unit's first
# start at 42.714s, so 7.386s later. All three from the same boot, which is what
# makes the interval mean something when the offsets themselves do not.
#
# **That is ONE data point, and #209 withdrew the paragraph that tried to make
# it more.** This file used to argue that boot -3 started unusually late at
# 22.7s while the other four started at 14.1s to 14.6s, and that an ordinary
# boot therefore reaches the bind about a second BEFORE the address exists.
# Every number in that sentence was measured against an unstable origin.
#
# Re-measured on `__MONOTONIC_TIMESTAMP`, the only origin comparable across
# boots, this unit starts 3 to 18 MILLISECONDS after `user@1000.service` on
# every one of six boots. Its start offset therefore records when the user
# manager came up and nothing else, and it cannot support any inference about
# boot ordering or network readiness. The 14.1s to 14.6s band is not
# reproducible under any origin, and "boot -3 started late" inverts once the
# origin is stable: at 42.7s it is the second earliest of the six. It looked
# late because that boot flushed its journal at 20.6s where others flushed
# anywhere from 14.5s to 66.3s, moving the apparent origin by up to 52 seconds.
#
# So the floor the test below asserts rests on ONE observation, and this file
# says so rather than generalising from it. What a budget does not have to
# outlast is absent hardware, which is what the withdrawn reasoning claimed it
# did.
#
# So 12 and 120 are arbitrary rather than derived. They are left alone because
# no measurement argues for another pair, and because the shipped `ExecStart`
# below binds loopback, which always exists, so none of this can reach the
# default install.
#
# **What is actually broken is #201:** a unit whose address appears AFTER the
# budget is spent stays dead, and on 2026-09-06 that was 3m25s of a working
# address with no service on it. A larger budget does not fix that. The fix
# under discussion there is socket activation with `FreeBind=yes`, which binds
# an address that is not there yet.
#
# **`FreeBind=yes` is not free**, and #201 carries the warning: it removes the
# `ip_nonlocal_bind=0` property that is currently the only reason this unit
# cannot bind an address that is not on this machine. It needs its own review.
#
# Raise the window with the burst if you raise either: the test asserts
# `burst * RestartSec` stays inside the window, or the limit can never fire and
# the loop is unbounded again.
StartLimitIntervalSec=120
StartLimitBurst=12

[Service]
# `uv tool install hitchrail`, not `uvx`. uvx resolves and runs out of a cache
# it is free to evict, so it is deliberately ephemeral and the path is not
# stable. A unit needs an executable that is still there next month.
ExecStart=%h/.local/bin/hitchrail --root main=%h/projects

# Mode 600, and this is not a style note. This file holds HITCHRAIL_TOKEN, and
# anyone who can read it can run code on this machine as you. A token in a
# mode 644 file under ~/.config is the failure the whole arrangement exists to
# avoid.
#
# It must hold a token. A generated one changes on every restart, so the link
# saved on your phone dies with each one, and the banner says so when it
# notices it is talking to the journal.
EnvironmentFile=%h/.config/hitchrail/env

# #145. Python block buffers stdout when it is not a terminal, and under a unit
# stdout is the journal. Hitchrail flushes its own banner, so this is not what
# makes that appear; it covers everything else the process writes to stdout for
# a service whose only output surface is a log you read later. The failure it
# prevents is silence, which reads as "it started fine" rather than as a
# missing message.
Environment=PYTHONUNBUFFERED=1

# **Without this line a lingering install is dead after its first reboot**
# (#195). Measured on a real boot: started at 17:23:06, refused at 17:23:06 with
# "'claude' is not on PATH", exit 2, and correctly stayed stopped.
#
# `loginctl enable-linger`, which step 4 of the README's setup tells you to
# run, starts the user manager AT BOOT, before any login. Its PATH then is systemd's
# fallback, `/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin`, and the agent lives in
# `~/.local/bin`, which is not in it.
#
# It hides in the way that costs the most: starting the unit by hand always
# happens after a graphical login has pushed the full PATH into the manager, so
# `systemctl --user show-environment` looks healthy and every interactive test
# passes. Reproduce the real condition without rebooting:
#
#   env -i HOME="$HOME" PATH=/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin \
#     ~/.local/bin/hitchrail --root main="$HOME/projects"
#
# **What this PATH definitely does is let Hitchrail RESOLVE the agent at
# startup.** Whether it becomes the agent's OWN path is a separate question with
# a surprising answer, and it is worth knowing before editing this line.
#
# A tmux pane inherits the environment of the SERVER, captured when the server
# started, not the environment of the client that asked for the pane. That is
# measured in `tmux.py` at #113, on tmux 3.4 against a pre existing server. So:
#
#   - At BOOT there is usually no server yet, Hitchrail starts one, and the
#     agent gets this line.
#   - If a server is already running from your login, panes get THAT server's
#     environment and this line does not reach them.
#
# So an agent may see a different PATH depending on who started the tmux server
# first, which is worth knowing and is not something this file can fix.
#
# **You may have to add to it, and probably not for the agent itself.** Claude
# Code is a self contained native executable however it was installed: the npm
# package ships the same binary and links it into place, so nothing here needs
# node or npm on your account. Another agent, set with `--agent-binary`, might
# be a launcher that needs an interpreter, and then its directory belongs here.
#
# **Your own projects are the usual reason to add one.** An agent working in a
# node project needs node on ITS path, and this line is that path at boot.
# Prefer a stable directory to a version pinned one: something like
# `%h/.nvm/versions/node/v22.22.2/bin` goes stale at the next upgrade and fails
# at the next BOOT rather than at the upgrade, which is a long way from the
# change that caused it.
Environment=PATH=%h/.local/bin:/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin

# **What restarts, what stays stopped, and what is merely bounded.** #170, and
# the comment that used to sit here got the middle one wrong.
#
# It said `on-failure, never always`, on the reasoning that a configuration
# refusal is a deliberate stop and must stay stopped. The reasoning is right
# and `on-failure` does not deliver it: it restarts on ANY non zero exit, and
# a refusal exits 2. Measured with an `EnvironmentFile` holding a blank token:
# 37 restarts and 38 copies of the same message, never rate limited, because
# `RestartSec=5` keeps the attempts outside systemd's default 5-in-10s window.
#
# Exit 2 is every deliberate stop, and it is not a number this file chose.
# `argparse` exits 2 on a usage error, which is what a typo in the ExecStart
# above produces, and `cli.main` returns 2 for a configuration it refuses.
# Changing it would let a mistyped flag loop forever on the one line these
# instructions tell you to edit.
RestartPreventExitStatus=2

# Exit 3 is uvicorn's startup failure, which is the port already being in use.
# That one is worth retrying: it is usually a previous instance still shutting
# down. It is NOT in the list above, deliberately.
#
# The old comment listed "a host it will not bind" among the refusals that must
# stay stopped. That was wrong twice: a wildcard bind is accepted rather than
# refused, and a bind that fails is exit 3, which this unit retries on purpose.
Restart=on-failure
RestartSec=5

[Install]
WantedBy=default.target
